.TH CCOL_CREATE_CTHREAD_POOL_MP 3 "2026-09-27" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_create_cthread_pool_mp \- create a thread pool with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

ctpool ccol_create_cthread_pool_mp(size_t num_threads,
                              size_t queue_capacity,
                              ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs,
                              char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new thread pool with the given number of worker threads.
The queue_capacity parameter selects the task queue mode:

  0 or ccol_invalid_size -> unbounded task queue. ctpool_submit never
    blocks on capacity. The only failure path that does not come from
    an argument is ccol_not_enough_memory.

  N > 0 -> bounded task queue of capacity N. ctpool_submit blocks when
    the queue is full. ctpool_try_submit does not block. It returns
    ccol_container_full.

This function starts all num_threads worker threads before it returns.
If a pthread_create call fails, it joins the threads that already
started, cleanly, and then it returns CTPOOL_INVALID.

Each worker thread records its pool on one thread-specific key that the
library creates for the whole process, on the first creation of a pool.
That key is what lets a task that destroys its own pool fail loudly. When
the process has no key left to give (PTHREAD_KEYS_MAX), this function
returns CTPOOL_INVALID, and so does every later call in that process.
The library deletes the key when it unloads with no pool left, so a
cycle of dlopen(3) and dlclose(3) gives the key back.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
num_threads    Number of worker threads (must be >= 1)
queue_capacity 0 or ccol_invalid_size for unbounded; N > 0 for bounded
mprocs         Custom allocator, or NULL for malloc/free
err_str        Optional. It gets a static error string on failure
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new ctpool handle on success.
Returns CTPOOL_INVALID on failure, and sets *err_str if err_str is
not NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The handle that this function gives is an opaque VALUE, and not a
pointer. Do not cast it to void* or from void*. Do not use it as an
address. Compare it with CTPOOL_INVALID. CTPOOL_INVALID is 0. Therefore, a
test such as if (!pool) is also correct.
A future always uses the standard malloc and free, whatever mprocs is.
Each submission has a small internal task node behind it. The library
recycles that node through a cache in each pool. It does not allocate
and free the node through mprocs on every call. A caller can therefore
not depend on mprocs to see one allocation and one free for each
submission. A tracking or measuring allocator is one such caller. Such
a caller sees a call only when the cache is empty or already full, and
when the pool trims the cache.
The cache keeps the nodes that the recent traffic of the pool needed,
so a burst of submissions of the same depth as an earlier one
allocates no node. It holds at most max(num_threads * 4, 32768) idle
nodes. At every 256th point where the pool goes idle, it gives back to
mprocs half of what it holds above num_threads * 4, unless the traffic
since the previous such point emptied it. A cache that nothing needs
therefore halves at each of those points, down to num_threads * 4.
ctpool_destroy does a drain shutdown itself, when the caller called
neither ctpool_shutdown_drain nor ctpool_shutdown_immediate before it.
ctpool_destroy waits for every in-flight resolved use of the handle to
stop, before it frees anything. Such a use is a ctpool_submit,
ctpool_wait or ctpool_pending_count call that runs at the same time.
The caller therefore does not need to prove on its own that no other
thread still uses the pool.

There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with cthreadpool. The
first is a fork(2) before the creation of a pool. The second is the
creation of a pool and then a fork(2) with an exec(3) right after it.
A child that keeps running with no exec(3) in between, while a pool
created before the fork is still live, is not a supported pattern.
On top of that, the library adds a standing defence. A fresh call to
ccol_create_cthread_pool_mp, ctpool_submit, ctpool_try_submit,
ctpool_timed_submit or ctpool_destroy, or to one of the future, wait
and shutdown variants, never hangs on a lock. This holds for a call
from any thread, in the parent or in a child that forked a moment ago.
The lock can belong to another thread that held it at the instant of
the fork. That thread may no longer exist, because fork(2) duplicates
only the thread that calls it.
A pool that is already live across the fork is a different matter,
whatever that defence does. The child does not get copies of its
worker threads. An inherited pool can therefore run no queued task and
no future task there. Treat such a pool as inert in the child, and do
not submit to it. ctpool_wait on such a pool in the child returns at
once. It does not wait for progress that can never happen.
ctpool_destroy still discards any work that was in the queue at the
moment of the fork. It cancels every queued future, so a caller that
is blocked in ctpool_future_get on one of them wakes up and does not
hang. ctpool_destroy does this before it frees the memory of the pool,
and it does it even with no explicit shutdown call before it.
.fi
